<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring My Community – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Market!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them. Interactive: Answer simple questions about a story they have just listened to.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate understanding of basic descriptions of people, places, and things around them (e.g., pointing to the park when listening "The park is next to the school."). Can answer simple questions about familiar stories just heard (e.g., "Where is the boy in the story?" "At the market."). Can identify simple characteristics of people and places in basic oral conversations or descriptions (e.g., "It's a big park." "Maria is tall and Jorge is short.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge and introduces core vocabulary for listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a brief "What do you see?" activity using colorful flashcards or pictures of community places. Flashcards include: 'market', 'park', 'school', 'church', 'library'. The teacher holds up a flashcard, points to it, and asks, "What's this?" encouraging students to point or give a single-word answer. The teacher then explicitly states, "It's the market!" or "It's the park!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of target vocabulary words like "market" /mɑːrkɪt/, "park" /pɑːrk/, and "church" /tʃɜːrtʃ/ emphasizing the initial sounds /m/, /p/, and /tʃ/. The teacher uses gestures (e.g., hands mimicking shopping for 'market', running for 'park') and repeats each word clearly 2-3 times. Students are encouraged to listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also models "It's a big market" and "It's a small park" using gestures to show size.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the newly introduced vocabulary through simple questions and visual cues.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up 'market' flashcard) "Is this a park or a market?" (Expected answer: "Market!" or pointing to the market).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing two pictures, one of a big market and one of a small park) "Is the market big or small?" (Expected answer: "Big!" or pointing to the 'big' market).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario: "Exploring My Community". Students listen to authentic input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Simple audio story: "A Day at the Market". The audio describes a child visiting a big, busy market and seeing a small park nearby. The language is kept very simple and repetitive, using target vocabulary (market, park, big, busy, next to).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (Listen for main idea):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Then asks, "What place are we listening about? The market or the school?" (Students point to 'market' flashcard or say "Market!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Listen for specific details):</b> The teacher plays the audio again, pausing after key phrases.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "The market is big." (Students show thumbs up for True or thumbs down for False).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "The park is next to the market." (Students show thumbs up/down, or point to "next to" gesture).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Listen and Point):</b> The teacher plays segments of the audio. After hearing a description, students point to the corresponding flashcard (e.g., when they hear "It's a big market!", they point to the 'market' flashcard. When they hear "The park is small and green," they point to the 'park' flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Students engage in controlled and semi-controlled activities to practice identifying specific language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for specific vocabulary (market, park, school, library, church, store, big, small, busy, quiet), WH-questions ("Where is it?"), Present simple ("It's the market."), and Adjectives + nouns ("big park"). Phonemic awareness: /p/, /m/, /ʈʃ/ (ch).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher places flashcards of 'market', 'park', 'school', 'church', 'library', 'store' on the board. The teacher then says a word or a simple phrase (e.g., "market", "big park", "small school"). Students listen and physically point to the correct flashcard on the board. The teacher can also say "Where is the market?" and students point.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Draw/Color (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a simple worksheet with outlines of various community places. The teacher gives short, descriptive instructions: "Draw a big market." "Color the church red." "Draw a small park next to the school." Students listen and follow the instructions. The teacher walks around, observing students' comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Listen and Find" with Partners (Peer Check - Listening Focus):</b> Each student has a set of small picture cards (market, park, school, big, small). The teacher calls out a description, e.g., "Find the big park!" or "Find the busy market!" Students listen and hold up the correct picture card. They then show their card to their partner, who confirms if it's correct. The teacher circulates, listening to student interactions and providing feedback. This activity also reinforces /p/ in park and /m/ in market.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring My Community". Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Community Map Listen and Locate". Students will collaboratively listen to descriptions and identify locations on a simple map.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> The teacher prepares a large, simple drawing or poster of a fictional community map, showing outlines of a 'market', 'park', 'school', 'church', 'library', 'store' with empty spaces. Students work in small groups (2-3 students) around the map. The teacher gives oral instructions describing the location of specific places using target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher starts: "Hello, children! Let's explore our community map!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 1: "Listen carefully: It's the market. It's big and busy." (Students listen and place a small 'market' picture/label on the map).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 2: "Where is the park? The park is small. It's next to the market." (Students listen and place the 'park' picture/label next to the 'market').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 3: "The school is big. It's near the park." (Students listen and place the 'school' picture/label).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 4: "The church has a 'ch' sound. It's quiet. It's next to the school." (Students listen for the description and initial sound, then place the 'church' picture/label).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to point and confirm with their group members. "Is the park next to the market?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This activity focuses on students' ability to comprehend oral descriptions and identify locations based on listened information, promoting active listening and collaborative problem-solving at a Pre-A1.4 level.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check learning and provide feedback. Includes student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note student performance during the "Listen and Match" activity, focusing on their ability to comprehend and respond to descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Match Pictures". Students receive a worksheet with 4-5 labeled pictures of community places (market, park, school, church, library). The teacher reads a short description for each place. Students listen and draw a line from the description number to the correct picture.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "Number 1: It's a big place. You can buy fruits here. It's the market."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "Number 2: It's green. You can play and run here. It's the park."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "Number 3: It has books. It's quiet. It's the library."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "Number 4: Children go here to learn. It's the school."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening Focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of target vocabulary (market, park, school, library, church, big, small, next to, near).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately identifies described places by matching them to pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds appropriately to simple WH-questions (e.g., by pointing or selecting the correct picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies initial sounds in spoken words (e.g., /m/ in market, /p/ in park, /tʃ/ in church).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer with a simple gesture (thumbs up/down or Yes/No circle on a paper).</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the word 'market' and point to it."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear 'big park' and find the big park."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Encourages students and teacher to reflect on the learning process and connect to future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about our community did you hear today?" (Teacher elicits responses like "market!", "park!", "big!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to? What was a little difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, repetition of target vocabulary and sounds like /m/ and /p/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more visual support for prepositions, slower audio pace, additional practice with 'church' /tʃ/ sound).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced key community vocabulary through listening. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will see these same vocabulary words in a simple written text and practice identifying them in context, reinforcing their understanding from today's listening activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>